    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOMMiGhs77krvY",
      "title": "fix: `SUPERSIM_INTEROP_L2TOL2CDM_OVERRIDE` env var is never matched",
      "author": "boleklebovski",
      "number": 423,
      "body": "## Problem\n\n`config/cli.go:70` registers the environment variable for the L2ToL2CrossDomainMessenger artifact override as:\n\n```go\nEnvVars: opservice.PrefixEnvVar(envPrefix, \"INTEROP_L2TO2CDM_OVERRIDE\"),\n```\n\n`L2TO2CDM` is missing an `L`. Every other spelling of this option in the codebase uses `l2tol2`:\n\n- the flag name, `config/cli.go:40` — `interop.l2tol2cdm.override`\n- the constant and struct field — `InteropL2ToL2CDMOverrideArtifactPath`\n\nSo a user who sets `SUPERSIM_INTEROP_L2TOL2CDM_OVERRIDE` — the spelling every other identifier implies — gets no effect at all, silently. The override path itself is live and used (`interop/applier.go:97-98`), so the failure mode is a custom artifact simply being ignored while supersim carries on with the default implementation.\n\n`docs/src/reference/supersim.md:82` currently reproduces the misspelling too, because it is generated from the flag definition, so it propagates the wrong name to anyone reading the docs.\n\n## Fix\n\nOne character in `config/cli.go`, plus the same correction in the generated reference doc so the two stay in sync.\n\n## Note on scope\n\nCONTRIBUTING asks contributors not to open spelling or grammar PRs. This is not that: the string is a functional identifier, and the misspelling means the documented environment variable never resolves. Happy to close it if you disagree.\n\n## Verification\n\n`grep` confirms `L2TO2CDM` no longer appears anywhere in the repo, and that the flag name, constant, struct field, env var and doc line now all agree on `l2tol2cdm`. The change is confined to a string literal, so it cannot affect compilation.",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/supersim",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-06T12:05:32Z",
      "mergedAt": null,
      "additions": 2,
      "deletions": 2
    }
  ],
